-> The Difference b/w Spring and Spring Boot:
    - In Spring we do configurations with xml; whereas in Spring Boot, we do configurations using annotations.
    - even Spring too supports annotations. and Spring Boot it completely works only with annotations.

// NOTE ---------
    ( Singleton Design Pattern:
            It says, 1 class -> 1 object (multiple objects should not be created for a single class,
                                          instead use a same object and share the same object to multiple requests)
            - for that we have to mention the constructor in the class as 'private' and we have to declare the object in the class
                and we create a public getter method and return that declared object following few rules like,
                putting a null check, where only if the check is null we create the object otherwise we return the existing object.
    The two rules -
        -> make the constructor 'private'
        -> write a static method which returns the instance.
        -> whenever we need object , we just call that static method.
    )
    // ----------

    -- "Spring entirely works on 'Singleton Design Pattern'" --
           -By default all classes in Spring are Singleton.

- Beans = Objects (in spring)

- Spring handles beans. i.e. objects are not created by us, instead spring does.

-> IOC - Inversion Of Control (as the control of handling bean(object) is inversed, it is called 'Inversion of Control')
    - Spring uses a container to manage this (IoC container). It stores Beans.

    -> 2 Types of containers :
        1. BeanFactory
        2. ApplicationContext (it is advanced version of BeanFactory. and we generally use ApplicationContext)
            - ApplicationContext is an Interface.
            - As it is an interface there are two classes which provides it's implementation.
                -> 1. ClassPathXMLApplicationContext - it is used when we are configuring everything using XML.
                   2. AnnotationConfigApplicationContext - it is used when we are configuring using annotations.

1. Spring Core / Context :-

    - we have to add the Spring Context dependency in pom.xml.
    - we do configurations using XML.
    - we create an .xml file(beans.xml) in src/main/resources (beans.xml should include a meta data which can be get from Spring XSD for beans.xml)
        inside beans.xml, inside <beans> </beans> tag, we write <bean> <bean> i.e.
            - <bean class="com.model.User" id="user">
                // -- by default single bean will be created in Spring. --
                // we can set values using 'property tag' or 'constructor-arg tag'
                // property tag -> uses setters to set the value
                // constructor-arg tag -> uses constructor to set the value
                // -- below ex belongs to Ex1 --
                <property name="userId" value="1"></property>
                <property name="name" value="Prasanth"></property>

                // we can write constructor-arg here too.

                <property name="skills">
                    <list>
                        <value>JAVA</value>
                        <value>SQL</value>
                        <value>JDBC</value>
                    </list>

                </property>

                // if we want to use map, we use entry set
                <property name="hobbies">
                    <map>
                        <entry key="Hobby1" value="Cricket"></entry>
                        <entry key="Hobby2" value="Drawing"></entry>
                        <entry key="Hobby3" value="Tennis"></entry>
                    </map>
                </property>

                // -------
                // for has-a relationship the property value will be 'ref' because it will be a class
                // has-a relation means, one class having field as another class object.
                <property name="address" ref="addr">>/property>
                // as the address itself is a class, we will create a bean as we did,
                // we pass that beans 'id' as 'ref'
                // this is a limitation, so we do 'Autowire'
                // -------

              </bean>

              // -- example of address bean --
              <bean>
                <property> //// </property>
              </bean>
              // ----------------

              // we can write multiple beans for same class with different 'id'
              // but this is wrong, and violates singleton design pattern, as it creates multiple beans
              <bean class="com.model.User" id="user2>
                // setting values using 'constructor-arg'
                <constructor-arg value="3"></constructor-arg>
                <constructor-arg value="XYZ"></constructor-arg>
              </bean>

--> Dependency Injection : Spring does it by 'Autowire'
                           Autowire -> automatically injecting another class obj in a class

            - Spring does Autowire in 3 ways :
                    1. byType
                    2. byName
                    3. constructor - when want to do autowire using constructor, we don't mention property tag.


-> Spring Context without XML :
    - as we mention the attribute "autowire" for DI, we replace it with '@Autowired' annotation.
    - when we use/see 'has-a' relationship, we use @Autowired.
    - we can put @Autowired - on 'setter', or on 'field', or on 'constructor'
    - -- we generally follow 'constructor' injection.
    - NOTE :
            In order to Spring understand that we using annotations,
                    we have to mention <context:annotation-config></context:annotation-config> tag.
                    with this tag, spring will know we are using annotations and looks for annotations.

    - We can set values dynamically by mentioning the values in 'application.properties' file and
        instead of hardcoding in the XML, we can pass as placeholders- "${id}" where 'id' is mentioned in the application.properties file.
        initially the placeholders will won't work, we have to use <context:property-placeholder location="classpath:application.properties"> in XML.
        still we are using beans tag. we can remove this too with @Component annotation.
    - we can use values dynamically using @Value() annotation - @Value("${id}") on field.

    - @Component replaces bean tag. -creates a bean.
    - @ComponentScan - to scan all Components
        - we can also configure it using XML too where it asks 'base package' tp look into for scanning components.

    - when wee configuring using annotations, we create a configuration class to enable all these annotations (say Config)
        - we mention @Configuration annotation on the Config class, this will enable annotation based configuration.
        - below it we mention the @ComponentScan(basePackages = "com")
        - and to enable placeholders we mention, @PropertySource("classpath:application.properties")    (for dynamically setting values)

    - Now, as we replaced all XML configurations with annotations, we can remove 'beans.xml' file.
        - As we are using annotations we have to change the container to - AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(Config.class);
            - as we enable the config in Config class , as we did in beans.xml for XML config so we mention Config.class in the container.

